 2. September spending slump 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Bureau of Economic Analysis ; Note: Adjusted for inflation using the PCE price index; Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios The economy entered the fall with considerable momentum. But a snapshot from September suggests that households ended the third quarter squeezed by higher prices and weaker purchasing power.

 Driving the news: Consumer spending was strong across the third quarter but showed signs of stalling in September in data released simultaneously with the GDP report this morning.

 By the numbers: Personal consumption expenditures rose 0.5% in September, slowing from August's 0.9% gain. But after accounting for a 0.5% rise in prices, September spending was flat after rising by 0.5% the prior month.

 Inflation-adjusted disposable income fell 0.3%, the steepest monthly decline since April. As spending outpaced income growth and inflation eroded purchasing power, the personal saving rate slipped to 3.8%, its lowest since October 2022. The intrigue: Inflation cooled over the third quarter, though price pressures were stubborn at the end of the three-month period.

 The core PCE price index rose at a 2.5% annualized rate last quarter, down from 3.3% in the second quarter. Headline inflation slowed to 2.1% from 5%. Headline prices rose 0.5%, up from 0.3% in August. Annual inflation accelerated to 3.6% from 3.4%, while core inflation ticked up to 3.1% from 3%. The bottom line: Together, the GDP and PCE reports capture a central tension in the U.S. economy ahead of next week's midterm elections.

 Consumer spending is powering robust growth, even as households contend with higher prices and declining purchasing power while saving a smaller share of their income. 
 
